# Random Number Generator Using a Vector in C++

Here are several approaches to generating random numbers and storing them in a `std::vector` in C++:

## Basic Approach Using `<random>` Library

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    // Set up random number generator
    std::random_device rd;              // Non-deterministic seed source
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());             // Mersenne Twister engine
    std::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(1, 100); // Range [1, 100]

    const int size = 10;
    std::vector<int> randomNumbers(size);

    // Fill vector with random numbers
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        randomNumbers[i] = dis(gen);
    }

    // Display the results
    std::cout << "Random numbers: ";
    for (const auto& num : randomNumbers) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## Alternative: Using `std::generate`

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());
    std::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(1, 100);

    const int size = 10;
    std::vector<int> randomNumbers(size);

    // Use std::generate to fill the vector
    std::generate(randomNumbers.begin(), randomNumbers.end(),
                  [&]() { return dis(gen); });

    // Display the results
    std::cout << "Random numbers: ";
    for (const auto& num : randomNumbers) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## Alternative: Generating Unique Random Numbers (No Duplicates)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());

    const int range = 100;   // Numbers from 1 to 100
    const int count = 10;    // Pick 10 unique numbers

    // Create a vector with all numbers in range
    std::vector<int> numbers(range);
    std::iota(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), 1); // Fill with 1..100

    // Shuffle the vector
    std::shuffle(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), gen);

    // Take the first 'count' elements
    std::vector<int> uniqueRandoms(numbers.begin(), numbers.begin() + count);

    // Display the results
    std::cout << "Unique random numbers: ";
    for (const auto& num : uniqueRandoms) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## Key Points

| Component | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `std::random_device` | Provides a non-deterministic seed for better randomness |
| `std::mt19937` | Mersenne Twister PRNG — high-quality, fast |
| `std::uniform_int_distribution` | Generates integers uniformly within a range |
| `std::uniform_real_distribution` | Use this for floating-point random numbers |
| `std::shuffle` + `std::iota` | Best way to get **unique** random selections |

## Why Not `rand()`?

- `rand()` is **legacy**, has a small range (`RAND_MAX`), and poor statistical properties.
- The `<random>` library provides **better distribution**, **reproducibility** (via fixed seeds), and **type safety**.

Choose the approach that fits your needs:
- **Duplicates allowed** → Use `std::generate` or a loop with `distribution`.
- **Unique values needed** → Use `std::shuffle` on a pre-filled vector.

Here is the CSS code for your `style.css` file.

I have selected **"Space Grotesk"** as the Google Font. It is a modern, geometric sans-serif that fits a tech/processor theme perfectly, and it is distinct from the forbidden fonts (Poppins, Open Sans, Montserrat, Roboto).

The header color is set to a **Slate Blue** (`#4a6fa5`), which sits perfectly between a neutral gray and a saturated blue, with a medium saturation level.

```css
/* Import Google Font: Space Grotesk */
@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;700&display=swap');

/* Global Reset and Body Styling */
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

body {
  font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;
  background-color: #f4f4f4; /* Light background for contrast */
  color: #333;
  line-height: 1.6;
  padding-bottom: 20px;
}

/* Header Styling */
header {
  /* Color: Between gray and saturated, medium shade of blue */
  background-color: #4a6fa5; 
  color: white;
  padding: 20px;
  position: relative; /* Allows positioning of the hanging CPU */
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 15px;
}

header a {
  color: white;
  text-decoration: none;
  font-weight: 700;
}

header a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

header img {
  max-height: 50px; /* Adjust logo size as needed */
  width: auto;
}

header span {
  font-size: 1.1rem;
}

/* Hanging CPU Image in Header */
.hanger {
  position: absolute;
  bottom: -30px; /* Hangs off the bottom */
  right: 20px;   /* Positioned on the right side */
  width: 60px;   /* Adjust size as needed */
  height: auto;
  z-index: 10;   /* Ensures it sits on top of content if overlapping */
}

/* Main Content Styling */
main {
  max-width: 800px;
  margin: 40px auto; /* Centers the content */
  padding: 0 20px;
  background-color: transparent; /* Ensures no background color */
}

h1 {
  margin-bottom: 20px;
  color: #2c3e50;
}

p {
  margin-bottom: 10px;
  font-size: 1.1rem;
}

